--TEST--
An outbound socket carries RFC 6455's two payload kinds each way, and the `Core\Socket\Message` it answers in fills exactly one of `text` and `bytes` — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-socket-is-opened-like-an-outbound-call`, `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of both directions: one shape carries a frame whichever end
// of the wire it came from, and which reader answers is what says which kind it
// was. A message class of its own for the outbound direction would differ only
// in where the octets came from, which is why there is not one.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/mixed", ["a text frame", "AB" as bytes]);

var $socket = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/mixed");

var $message = $socket->receive();
while ($message != null) {
    // `topic` and `value` are a delivery's, so a frame off a wire answers
    // `null` for both — a peer frame and a published one are one class and the
    // topic is what tells them apart.
    echo "peer ";
    if ($message->text() != null) {
        echo "text ", $message->text() ?? "-";
    } else {
        echo "bytes ", Core\Bytes::length($message->bytes() ?? ("" as tainted bytes));
    }
    if ($message->topic() == null) {
        echo " topic=null";
    }
    echo "\n";
    $message = $socket->receive();
}

// The same pair going the other way, and read back in the order they were
// written rather than grouped by kind.
$socket->sendBytes("CDE" as bytes);
$socket->send("and text");
$socket->close();

foreach (Core\Test::sentSocket() as Core\Socket\Message $frame) {
    if ($frame->text() != null) {
        echo "sent text ", $frame->text() ?? "-", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "sent bytes ", Core\Bytes::length($frame->bytes() ?? ("" as tainted bytes)), "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
peer text a text frame topic=null
peer bytes 2 topic=null
sent bytes 3
sent text and text
